\section{Checking Values Rather than Only Locations}

A checker for register allocation must establish that instructions
read the intended values, not merely that no two live intervals
share a color. It must also account for inserted moves, spill
stores and reloads, entry arguments, call clobbers and results,
and value transfers on individual control-flow edges. Rideau and
Leroy's \emph{Validating Register Allocation and Spilling} matches
source operations to transformed operations and infers equations
between virtual values and locations by backward dataflow. The
equations are obligations: at each point they must hold to justify
the remainder of execution. At a match for $v\gets f(x)$ whose
allocated counterpart is $r_2\gets f(r_1)$, the operation can
discharge $v=r_2$ and require $x=r_1$ instead. But if another
obligation requires old $z=r_2$ after the instruction, the write
invalidates it unless the checker has an independent justification.

For a spill test, let a future use require $v=r_3$ after
$r_3\gets S(8,4)$, a load from a four-byte private slot at offset
8. Backward through the load the obligation is $v=S(8,4)$.
Now suppose an intervening store to $S(10,2)$ writes an unrelated
value. The slots have different names, but their byte ranges
overlap, so the store cannot be passed with that obligation intact.
A checker that treats stack slots as opaque, necessarily distinct
identifiers would miss the wrong reload. Apply the same reasoning
to overlapping register names, using instruction-specific write
effects. At a fork both reachable continuations must be justified;
edge transfers are accounted for before requirements are combined
at their common predecessor.

\section{Checking an Order and Its Machine Realization}

For pure local reordering, check that the result is a permutation
of the original instruction instances and respects every edge of
a sound dependence graph. A numbering by opcode is insufficient:
two loads of the same form can have different addresses and
destination identities. Yang, Shirako, and Sarkar's \emph{Fully
Verified Instruction Scheduling} separates a semantic proof for
swapping independent adjacent instructions from proving that a
new order respects the graph. To see the connection, bring the
first instruction in a proposed topological order to the front
of the old order. Any instruction it passes cannot be a required
predecessor, since it is first in the new order, or a successor,
since that would already contradict the old order. Repeat on the
suffix. This yields a sequence of justified adjacent swaps.

The graph itself must be sound. If two instructions store to
$p$ and then load from an address $q$ that can equal $p$, swapping
them can change the returned value despite disjoint virtual
register names. Rebuilding the graph from the physical
instructions must also include new anti-dependences introduced by
register reuse. And if bundles or exposed timing matter, replay
issue slots, result latencies, and resource reservations from a
trusted target description. For example, a consumer issued two
cycles after a three-cycle load may respect the graph's untimed
topological order yet violate the target timing constraint.
Speculation or compensation changes which instruction instances
execute; it requires path and effect reasoning beyond a
permutation test.

\section{Failure Localization and Recovery}

Give the checker concrete checkpoints: value and block identities
from the original IR, a map to transformed instructions, edge
copies, and, when relevant, issue cycles and instruction forms.
Treat these as claims to verify, not trusted proofs. If checking
after both passes is awkward because of inserted spills and
reordering, check allocation against its unscheduled input, then
check that reordering preserves the meaning of the allocated
stream. If the optimizer transforms both at once, it must provide
an equivalent decomposition or a checker able to relate the two
programs directly. A failing obligation should name the first
missing value, overwritten location, or resource conflict and
the edge or cycle where it occurs.

Test the checker by mutating valid outputs: remove one reload,
swap two locations on a single phi edge, clobber a preserved
register on just one return path, or advance an operation into a
busy slot. Each mutation has a predicted reason for rejection.
If checking fails in production, a conservative candidate can
be emitted only after it passes the applicable checks. Compare
Chapter~\ref{ch:translation-validation} and the dossier's
\emph{Transactional Compiler Transformations}: publish a
candidate after validation, not merely because the optimizer or
solver returned it.

